// Module 06, Exercise 02: strlen, And What Happens Without A Terminator
//
// In C, strlen walks the string one byte at a time until it finds the zero:
//
//   size_t strlen(const char *s) {
//       size_t n = 0;
//       while (s[n] != 0) n++;
//       return n;
//   }
//
// That is the entire implementation. Note what it does NOT do: it never
// checks how big the buffer is, because it cannot. A pointer carries no size.
// The terminator is the only thing standing between this loop and the rest of
// memory.

fn strlen(p) {
    n = 0
    while p[n] != 0 {
        n = n + 1
    }
    return n
}

// A properly terminated string: "HI" in three bytes.
good = garrison(3)
good[0] = 72
good[1] = 73
good[2] = 0

bathysphere(good)
print(f"strlen = {strlen(good)}")

// The same two letters, with no room for a terminator.
bad = garrison(2)
bad[0] = 72
bad[1] = 73

print("")
bathysphere(bad)

// Uncomment the next line to walk it:
// print(f"strlen = {strlen(bad)}")

scorch(good)
scorch(bad)

// YOUR TASK:
// 1. Run the file as-is. strlen(good) should be 2 — the terminator is not
//    counted, so a 3-byte block holds a 2-character string.
// 2. Uncomment the strlen(bad) line. What fault do you get, and which line
//    of strlen does it name?
// 3. The fault reports a byte index. What is it, and why that one?
// 4. In real C there is no fault here. strlen keeps reading past the block
//    into whatever comes next. What would it return? What would make it
//    stop?

// BONUS: strlen costs one full pass over the string every time you call it.
// Look at the loop and work out what `while (i < strlen(s))` costs — it is a
// classic accidentally-quadratic bug.
